1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,459
So what's the real trick…

2
00:00:01,538 --> 00:00:04,751
…to remembering what you read?

3
00:00:04,831 --> 00:00:08,272
Close the book, and it's all gone.

4
00:00:08,483 --> 00:00:14,188
Rereading feels familiar, "yeah, I know this…" but you can't explain it.

5
00:00:14,587 --> 00:00:19,121
When you reread, the words are right there. Nothing makes you recall.

6
00:00:19,521 --> 00:00:25,041
There's a real study: 2006, 120 students.

7
00:00:25,442 --> 00:00:31,025
Reread one passage, close the other, recall it from the title.

8
00:00:31,425 --> 00:00:37,122
Tested after 5 minutes: rereading wins. Tested after a week: it flips.

9
00:00:37,523 --> 00:00:44,673
Recall: 56% of ideas. Reread: 42%. Still not everything.

10
00:00:45,073 --> 00:00:48,599
So the skill to train: recalling with the book closed.

11
00:00:48,999 --> 00:00:56,784
First, like last video: finish a chapter, close the book, write what you remember from the title.

12
00:00:57,183 --> 00:01:00,307
Like me: write whatever you can recall.

13
00:01:00,707 --> 00:01:05,558
Second: only after writing, open the book and check. Fix what's wrong.

14
00:01:05,959 --> 00:01:09,281
Self-grading: no one's writing you up.

15
00:01:09,682 --> 00:01:13,938
This step is from the handbook, not the study.

16
00:01:14,638 --> 00:01:19,254
And finally: no peeking before checking. One peek and you're rereading.

17
00:01:19,953 --> 00:01:26,509
"You! Close the book and write what you remember! Then open it, grade yourself!"

18
00:01:26,909 --> 00:01:30,828
"Just a few mistakes, all fixed…"

19
00:01:31,227 --> 00:01:34,016
Remembering = quizzing yourself.

20
00:01:34,417 --> 00:01:41,037
You probably have better tricks than me. What helps you remember? Let me know in the comments.
